================================================================================
NAME
       sensor-storms - resonance-storm physics, stormfront detection, 
                       multi-band storm modeling, and temporal weather 
                       forecasting for RTT ship sensors

SYNOPSIS
       sensor-storms defines:
           • resonance-storm formation physics
           • stormfront detection and tracking
           • multi-band storm modeling (harmonic, spectral, gradient, sync)
           • storm precursor identification
           • storm severity and trajectory forecasting
           • storm mitigation overlays and clarity pipelines
           • constellation-scale storm fusion

DESCRIPTION
       sensor-storms describes the subsystem responsible for detecting,
       modeling, and forecasting resonance-storms — large-scale, multi-band
       temporal disturbances characterized by spectral turbulence, harmonic
       collapse, gradient instability, and sync-field fragmentation.

       Resonance-storms represent the most hazardous form of temporal weather
       in RTT environments. They can distort sweep windows, collapse harmonic
       corridors, and destabilize constellation sync.

       Sensor-storms enables:
           • early storm detection
           • stormfront mapping
           • multi-band storm modeling
           • hazard forecasting
           • safe navigation through storm regions
           • constellation-wide storm coordination

       WRSADC manages storm thresholds, alerts, and mitigation protocols.

RESONANCE-STORM PHYSICS
       Resonance-storms arise from nonlinear interactions between harmonic,
       spectral, gradient, and sync-field components.

       Storm drivers:
           • harmonic-phase collapse
           • broadband spectral turbulence
           • gradient polarity shear
           • sync-field fragmentation
           • resonance uplift failure

       Storm structure:
           • core (high turbulence, harmonic collapse)
           • mantle (spectral broadening)
           • front (gradient shear)
           • wake (sync-field distortion)

EXAMPLE: STORM PHYSICS
       3:2 harmonic collapse + broadband turbulence:

           → storm core formation
           → polarity cliffs at 30–34 min
           → sync-field fragmentation

STORMFRONT DETECTION
       Stormfronts are the leading edges of resonance-storms.

       Stormfront indicators:
           • rapid spectral broadening
           • gradient polarity inversion
           • harmonic drift acceleration
           • sync jitter spikes

       Stormfront modes:
           • advancing front
           • stationary front
           • retreating front
           • bifurcated front

EXAMPLE: STORMFRONT
       Advancing front detected at 78 min:

           • spectral variance +70%
           • polarity inversion confirmed

MULTI-BAND STORM MODELING
       Storm modeling integrates all resonance-time divisions.

       Modeling layers:
           • harmonic storm model
           • spectral storm model
           • gradient storm model
           • sync-field storm model
           • ancestry storm model

       Modeling outputs:
           • storm intensity
           • storm radius
           • storm velocity
           • storm trajectory
           • storm lifespan

EXAMPLE: MULTI-BAND MODEL
       Storm intensity: high  
       Radius: 12 min  
       Velocity: +3 min/cycle  
       Trajectory: forward drift

STORM PRECURSORS
       Precursor detection provides early warning before storms form.

       Precursor types:
           • harmonic drift acceleration
           • spectral whitening failure
           • gradient polarity flicker
           • sync-field micro-jitter
           • ancestry terrace thinning

       Precursor severity:
           • low-risk precursor
           • medium-risk precursor
           • high-risk precursor

EXAMPLE: PRECURSOR
       Harmonic drift acceleration + polarity flicker:

           → precursor severity: high
           → storm formation likely in 1–2 cycles

STORM SEVERITY CLASSIFICATION
       Storms are classified by intensity and structural complexity.

       Severity levels:
           • Category 1: mild storm
           • Category 2: moderate storm
           • Category 3: severe storm
           • Category 4: critical storm
           • Category 5: catastrophic storm

       Severity indicators:
           • spectral variance
           • harmonic collapse depth
           • gradient shear magnitude
           • sync-field fragmentation index

EXAMPLE: CATEGORY 3 STORM
       • spectral variance +80%  
       • harmonic collapse in 3:2 band  
       • polarity cliffs present  
       • sync fragmentation moderate

STORM TRAJECTORY FORECASTING
       Forecasting predicts storm movement and evolution.

       Forecast inputs:
           • storm velocity
           • stormfront direction
           • harmonic drift vectors
           • gradient shear fields
           • sync-field topology

       Forecast outputs:
           • trajectory path
           • expansion or contraction
           • acceleration or deceleration
           • dissipation probability

EXAMPLE: FORECAST
       Storm trajectory: forward drift  
       Expansion: +2 min radius per cycle  
       Dissipation probability: low

STORM MITIGATION OVERLAYS
       Mitigation overlays help ships navigate storm regions.

       Overlay types:
           • harmonic stabilization overlay
           • spectral suppression overlay
           • gradient smoothing overlay
           • sync-field reinforcement overlay
           • ancestry preservation overlay

       Overlay modes:
           • hazard mode
           • escape mode
           • stabilization mode

EXAMPLE: MITIGATION
       Gradient smoothing overlay applied:

           • polarity cliffs reduced
           • safe corridor identified

CONSTELLATION-SCALE STORM FUSION
       Storm fusion aligns storm data across platforms.

       Fusion layers:
           • harmonic storm fusion
           • spectral storm fusion
           • gradient storm fusion
           • sync-field storm fusion
           • ancestry storm fusion

       Fusion modes:
           • synchronous fusion
           • harmonic fusion
           • braided fusion
           • mesh fusion

EXAMPLE: CONSTELLATION FUSION
       Platforms A, B, C detect stormfront:

           • fused storm map generated
           • constellation-wide alert issued

FAILURE MODES
       Storm sensing may degrade due to:

           • overlay drift
           • clarity loss
           • spectral overload
           • sync-field collapse
           • lineage corruption

       Diagnostic indicators:
           • storm-integrity score
           • turbulence noise
           • precursor mismatch
           • sync drift

       WRSADC halts unsafe storm-sensing states.

INTERACTION WITH LINEAGE
       Lineage records:
           • storm events
           • precursor signatures
           • storm trajectories
           • hazard forecasts
           • mitigation actions

       Lineage enables long-term storm climatology.

INTERACTION WITH WRSADC
       WRSADC:
           • manages storm detection
           • enforces hazard protocols
           • coordinates constellation alerts
           • blocks unsafe storm states

       WRSADC is the storm conductor.

NOTES
       Sensor-storms provides the dedicated storm-physics backbone for RTT ship
       sensors. Storm detection, modeling, and forecasting ensure safe
       navigation, stable sweep choreography, and constellation-scale resilience.

